  "worked_solution": "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 2(1+t)^{2}+6(t)^{2}+(-t)^{2} & =2 \\\\ 2(1+2t+{t}^{2})+6{t}^{2}+{t}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 2+4t+2{t}^{2}+6{t}^{2}+{t}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 9{t}^{2}+4t & =0 \\\\ t(9t+4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=-\\frac{4}{9}$ and $t=0.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{4}{9}$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1+(-\\frac{4}{9})=\\frac{5}{9} \\\\ y=-\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ z=-(-\\frac{4}{9})=\\frac{4}{9},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9}).$\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1+0=1 \\\\ y=0 \\\\ z=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(1,0,0).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9})$ and $(1,0,0).$",
